Here are five points covering how the site was built, focused on the web engineering (no data-structure internals):\
\
**1. Design system defined before any experiment**\
The visual language lives in one place: `app/globals.css` holds the palette (`#3d6bff`, `#3ddc97`, `#ffb547`, `#ff5c7a` on black), the font stack (self-hosted Inter + JetBrains Mono via `@fontsource`), and a reusable glass layer \'97 `.glass-panel`, `.glass-tile`, `.glass-well`, `.sheen`, `.grid-dots`, `.range-slider`, plus keyframes for ambient motion. On top of that, `components/sections/flight/ui.tsx` exposes shared primitives (`GlassPanel`, `ToolButton`, `IconButton`, `Field`, `Badge`, `Metric`, `ExperimentHeader`, `SpaceBackdrop`). Building these first meant all eight experiments inherit the same rhythm, and a fix \'97 dark form fields, slider thumb colour, button contrast over artwork \'97 is applied once instead of eight times.\
\
**2. A single-scroll narrative with an isolated module inside it**\
The site is a Next.js 16 App Router project with one page composed of independent sections: `TopBar \uc0\u8594  Hero \u8594  Mission \u8594  Challenge \u8594  Build \u8594  FlightEngine \u8594  Footer`, stitched together with Lenis smooth scrolling and `scrollRestoration` disabled so refreshes always start at the top. The Hero is a `react-three-fiber` scene driven by scroll progress. Flight Core is deliberately treated as a module, not another section: its own header, engine-status pill, eight-tab rail, and GSAP stage transitions, with each experiment owning its state so switching tabs never leaks timers or stale animation state.\
\
**3. A typed data layer with a CORS-safe fallback path**\
Live telemetry comes through `lib/satnogsClient.ts` (`fetchSatnogsTelemetry`, `fetchSatnogsExp1` with cursor pagination), returning typed packet shapes rather than raw JSON. Because the Python backend at `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` does not send CORS headers, the browser never calls it directly \'97 Next.js route handlers (`/api/satnogs/telemetry`, `/api/exp1/satnogs/fetch`, `/api/exp4/status`) act as a server-side proxy. Every experiment degrades gracefully: if the link is down it keeps its local simulation running, and the header chip reflects the real state (`SATNOGS LINK`, `INGESTING DOWNLINK`, `Querying`) instead of failing silently.\
\
**4. Motion built as state machines, not decoration**\
Animation is the interface. GSAP (`useGSAP` scopes, `ScrollTrigger`) handles section reveals and stage changes, while each simulation is driven by explicit state \'97 a queue of steps, a timeline, a frame clock \'97 so buttons can interrupt, replay, or reset cleanly. Camera and viewport behaviour is computed rather than hard-coded (e.g. auto-fit via `ResizeObserver`, panning only when the active element would leave the frame). The quicksort audio is synthesised with the Web Audio API in `lib/sortAudio.ts` (no asset downloads), and every animated path respects the `prefers-reduced-motion` guard.\
\
**5. Iterated in the browser with a hard verification gate**\
The design was not written once and shipped. Each experiment was driven in a live dev server at real viewport sizes, screenshotted, and corrected: contrast on buttons sitting over the header artwork, amber saturation for the "buffer full" state, larger readable nodes, step-by-step instead of instant search, and a naming pass that renamed experiments to match what they actually show. Every iteration ended with `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, and `next build`, plus a console check to catch regressions \'97 and generated decoration (star fields, layouts) uses seeded, deterministic values so server and client markup match and hydration stays clean.\
